  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1209 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nModels in good agreement through Friday, with differences\nshowing up for the weekend into early next week. The ECMWF is\ndeeper with a trough over the weekend, with chances for\nprecipitation, while the GFS is more zonal with higher wind\npotential.\n\nWednesday through Friday...\n\nUpper level ridging will shift eastward across Colorado on\nWednesday. This will keep dry conditions over the area through\nWednesday night. Temperatures will continue to warm, with upper\n40s to mid 50s across the Plains, and mid 40s over the San Luis\nValley. Overnight lows will fall back into the 20s over the\nPlains, and teens for the San Luis Valley.\n\nA quick moving embedded shortwave will drop southeast across\nColorado on Thursday. No moisture to work with, so continued dry\nconditions will prevail. Higher elevations could see gusty winds\nThursday afternoon as the shortwave passes overhead. Broad\nnorthwest flow fills in behind the shortwave, and will continue\ninto Friday. High temperatures on Thursday will remain in the\n50s across the Plains, and upper 40s for the San Luis Valley. As\nnorthwesterly flow increases Thursday night into Friday morning,\nlee slopes will remain gusty, with temperatures in the 30s and\n40s, with 20s across the Plains. Continued dry conditions are\nforecast for Friday, and enhanced mixing will help boost\ntemperatures into the 60s by the afternoon. It will be breezy,\nespecially along the I-25 corridor in gap wind prone areas.\nGusts near 30 to 35 mph may be possible.\n\nSaturday through Monday...large discrepancies between the\nguidance for the weekend into early next week.  \n\nThe ECMWF drops a sharp shortwave trough across Colorado on\nSaturday, bringing with it a round of precipitation for the\nMountains, spreading into the I-25 corridor overnight into\nSunday morning. This solution seems to be the outlier compared\nto the GFS, various AI guidance and ensemble solutions.  \n\nThe GFS and other guidance has all trough activity well north,\nwith continued dry conditions across Colorado. The NBM guidance\nis similar, with just light shower activity possible over the\nweekend. The GFS guidance would be windy, with some strong wind\ngusts possible Saturday night in the lee of the Eastern\nMountains. Temperatures would likely be warmer than the NBM\nguidance which has 50s to near 60 Saturday through Monday.  \n\nGiven the uncertainty, the NBM guidance seems reasonable,\nhowever, winds, temperatures and pops may need to be adjusted as\nguidance keys in on a solution.",
